.\" robodr-anchor-receiver(8). Source of truth: the module docs of
.\" crates/robodr-blackbox/src/bin/robodr-anchor-receiver.rs and
.\" crates/robodr-blackbox/src/remote_anchor.rs. Keep them in sync.
.TH ROBODR\-ANCHOR\-RECEIVER 8 "2026-10-18" "RoboDR 0.1.0-alpha.1" "RoboDR Manual"
.SH NAME
robodr\-anchor\-receiver \- off\-unit home of black\-box head marks (remote head anchor)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B robodr\-anchor\-receiver serve
.B \-\-config
.I FILE
.br
.B robodr\-anchor\-receiver ingest
.B \-\-config
.I FILE
.IR MARKFILE ...
.br
.B robodr\-anchor\-receiver status
.B \-\-config
.I FILE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs on a base station or fleet host, never on the robot it anchors. Each
robot's black box (\fBrobodr\-agentd\fR(8) with
\fB"head_anchor": {"kind": "remote"}\fR) sends its head marks, signed with
the black\-box Ed25519 key; the receiver verifies them under the robot's
public key and keeps, per robot, the highest mark durably (atomic replace
and fsync before it answers). At start the robot asks for the held mark
with a fresh nonce; the answer is signed with the receiver's own Ed25519
key, and the robot checks it and the mark (under its own key). A robot
whose log was cut and whose state files were rolled back therefore reports
the truncation at its next start.
.PP
A mark lower than the held one, a second mark at the held position with
another hash (fork) and a mark whose signature does not verify are refused
and recorded as alerts: a line on stderr (the journal), a line in
.IB state_dir /alerts.jsonl
(bounded at 16 MiB) and a per\-robot counter. A stored state file that does
not verify at start blocks that robot (every request refused) until an
operator investigates; it is never reset silently.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B serve
Listen on \fBlisten\fR (one JSON request line per connection,
protocol \fBrobodr\-anchor/v1\fR, at most 16 KiB; slow peers are cut after
\fBio_timeout_ms\fR; at most \fBmax_connections\fR at a time). Prints
\fBlistening on ADDR\fR on stdout once bound.
.TP
.B ingest
Apply mark files written on a robot by
.B robodr\-blackbox anchor\-mark
and carried one way (diode, media), with the same rule. Detect\-only: the
robot never learns of a rollback, the receiver does. Needs the state
directory's lock (not while \fBserve\fR runs on it).
.TP
.B status
Print, as JSON, the held mark and alert count per robot. Read\-only; works
while \fBserve\fR runs.
.SH CONFIGURATION
JSON, unknown fields refused, paths absolute:
.PP
.nf
{"listen": "0.0.0.0:7461",
 "state_dir": "/var/lib/robodr\-anchor\-receiver",
 "key_file": "/run/credentials/robodr\-anchor\-receiver.service/receiver.key",
 "units": [{"unit": "ugv\-07",
            "public_key": "/etc/robodr\-anchor\-receiver/units/ugv\-07.pub"}],
 "max_connections": 64, "max_connections_per_source": 8, "io_timeout_ms": 5000,
 "tls": {"cert_chain": "/etc/robodr\-anchor\-receiver/tls/receiver.pem",
         "key_file": "/run/credentials/robodr\-anchor\-receiver.service/receiver\-tls.key",
         "client_ca": "/etc/robodr\-anchor\-receiver/tls/robots\-ca.pem"}}
.fi
.PP
\fBkey_file\fR is an Ed25519 secret key file (\fBrobodr\-blackbox keygen\fR,
mode 0600); the robots get its \fB.pub\fR. \fBunit\fR is the robot id;
\fBpublic_key\fR the robot's black\-box public key file (a key bound to a
device must name that robot).
.PP
\fBserve\fR speaks TLS: \fBtls.cert_chain\fR and \fBtls.key_file\fR are the
receiver's certificate chain and key (key: no access for others, no group
write); with \fBtls.client_ca\fR every robot must present a client
certificate chaining to it (mutual TLS). Without \fBtls\fR, \fBserve\fR exits
2 unless \fB"allow_plaintext": true\fR is set (lab or private link only);
both together are refused. \fBio_timeout_ms\fR bounds a whole connection,
TLS handshake included; \fBmax_connections_per_source\fR (default 8) caps the
connections one address may hold in each pool. Connections that have not
completed the mutual\-TLS handshake have \fBmax_connections\fR slots of
their own (a full pool evicts its least\-progress connection for a newcomer
instead of closing the newcomer) and units past it as many again; the
ClientHello must arrive within \fBhandshake_hello_timeout_ms\fR (default
1000) and the handshake keep \fBhandshake_min_bytes_per_s\fR (default 128)
after that. Optional: \fBmax_connections_per_subnet\fR (IPv4 /24, IPv6 /64;
default off), \fBpriority_sources\fR (CIDRs) with \fBpriority_connections\fR
(default 4) reserved slots, \fBhandshake_rate_per_s\fR (default 0, off).
Handshake pressure is logged at most once a minute. Key, certificate and CA
files must be owned by root or the service user.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
\fBingest\fR: at least one mark was refused (an alert was recorded).
.TP
.B 2
Usage, configuration, key, state or I/O error.
.SH NOTES
Marks and answers are authenticated with Ed25519 whatever the transport; TLS
adds confidentiality and, with \fBclient_ca\fR, keeps unknown hosts out. A
network attacker can drop marks or answers (the robot counts anchor
failures) but cannot make the receiver go back or a robot accept a lower
mark. Mutual TLS authenticates hosts, not unit ids; no CRL or OCSP check.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR robodr\-agentd (8),
.BR robodr\-blackbox (1)
